---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const threads = [
  {
    key: "search",
    label: "Search surface",
    tension: "People searched with partial titles, acronyms, publisher names, policy terms, and imperfect recall.",
    owned: "Built search and result surfaces around Elasticsearch-backed indexes, fuzzy matching, ranking, and filters.",
    proof: "Search became a practical route through a complex compliance dataset rather than a thin lookup box.",
  },
  {
    key: "docs",
    label: "Content system",
    tension: "Documentation needed to stay editable for content teams without becoming opaque to developers.",
    owned: "Built a Markdown-to-AST-to-React parser and patched in connection blocks for easier editing.",
    proof: "The repo could remain the source of truth while content synced into Directus for headless-CMS-style publishing.",
  },
  {
    key: "data",
    label: "Data quality",
    tension: "The dataset looked usable until somebody actually verified it.",
    owned: "Led the data-quality effort with the data team: massaging records, checking percentage errors, and tracing missing or incorrect fields.",
    proof: "Reduced glaring missing or incorrect data failures from roughly 70% to zero before launch.",
  },
];

const mechanisms = [
  {
    thread: "search",
    layer: "Search index",
    detail: "Elasticsearch, fuzzy search, ranking, filters, and result shaping.",
  },
  {
    thread: "data",
    layer: "Data quality",
    detail: "Verification passes, percentage-error checks, record cleanup, and source-data feedback.",
  },
  {
    thread: "docs",
    layer: "Content model",
    detail: "Markdown source, AST parsing, React rendering, connection blocks, and Directus sync.",
  },
  {
    thread: "docs",
    layer: "Documentation",
    detail: "Help and API-facing documentation for users, stakeholders, and consumers.",
  },
];

const initialThread = "search";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Open Policy Finder - Owen Abel Amenze"
  description="A case study on Open Policy Finder work across search, documentation, content systems, and data quality."
>
  <article class="case-note" data-case-note data-active-thread={initialThread}>
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/work">Work</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span>Open Policy Finder</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="case-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study</p>
      <h1>Open Policy Finder</h1>
      <p>
        Work on a Jisc product surface where open access compliance depended on
        policy, journal, and transitional-agreement data being searchable,
        legible, and trusted.
      </p>
    </header>

    <dl class="case-meta" aria-label="Case note summary">
      <div>
        <dt>Context</dt>
        <dd>Jisc / 2023-26</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Focus</dt>
        <dd>Search, documentation, content systems, Elasticsearch, indexing, data quality</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Link</dt>
        <dd>
          <a href="https://openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk">openpolicyfinder.jisc.ac.uk</a>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="case-tension">
      <h2 id="case-tension">Tension</h2>
      <p>
        Open access compliance is practical work. Users arrive with questions
        about policies, journals, agreements, and eligibility, while internal
        teams need documentation that can change safely. The interface had to
        support fuzzy, acronym-heavy search behaviour and a content workflow that
        stayed editable without hiding the shape of the underlying data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="case-threads">
      <h2 id="case-threads">Threads</h2>
      <div class="case-thread-grid">
        {
          threads.map((thread) => (
            <button
              class="case-thread"
              type="button"
              data-thread={thread.key}
              data-active={thread.key === initialThread ? true : undefined}
              aria-pressed={thread.key === initialThread ? "true" : "false"}
            >
              <span>{thread.label}</span>
              <small>{thread.tension}</small>
            </button>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="case-owned">
      <h2 id="case-owned">Owned</h2>
      <table class="case-ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Thread</th>
            <th scope="col">Work</th>
            <th scope="col">Signal</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            threads.map((thread) => (
              <tr data-thread={thread.key} data-active={thread.key === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
                <td>{thread.label}</td>
                <td>{thread.owned}</td>
                <td>{thread.proof}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="case-mechanism">
      <h2 id="case-mechanism">Mechanism</h2>
      <table class="case-ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Layer</th>
            <th scope="col">Detail</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            mechanisms.map((mechanism) => (
              <tr data-thread={mechanism.thread} data-active={mechanism.thread === initialThread ? true : undefined}>
                <td>{mechanism.layer}</td>
                <td>{mechanism.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-flow" aria-labelledby="case-shape">
      <h2 id="case-shape">Shape</h2>
      <ol>
        <li data-thread="search" data-active>A user starts with a policy or journal question.</li>
        <li data-thread="search" data-active>Fuzzy search and ranking narrow the problem into inspectable results.</li>
        <li data-thread="docs">Markdown content becomes structured React surfaces through an AST parser.</li>
        <li data-thread="docs">Connection blocks keep related guidance editable for content teams and developers.</li>
        <li data-thread="data">Verification catches missing and incorrect fields that had previously gone unnoticed.</li>
        <li data-thread="data">Data cleanup brings the failure rate from roughly 70% down to zero.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="case-reflection">
      <h2 id="case-reflection">Reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The important part was not only building search pages. It was making a
        compliance product easier to operate and easier to trust, from fuzzy
        search and ranking through to the repo-backed content model, Directus
        sync, documentation, and the verification work that made the underlying
        records safe to expose.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <script>
    const caseNote = document.querySelector("[data-case-note]");
    const controls = caseNote.querySelectorAll(".case-thread[data-thread]");
    const related = caseNote.querySelectorAll("[data-thread]");

    const setThread = (key) => {
      const nextKey = caseNote.dataset.activeThread === key ? undefined : key;
      caseNote.dataset.activeThread = nextKey ?? "";

      related.forEach((node) => {
        const active = Boolean(nextKey) && node.dataset.thread === nextKey;
        node.toggleAttribute("data-active", active);

        if (node.matches(".case-thread")) {
          node.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(active));
        }
      });
    };

    controls.forEach((control) => {
      control.addEventListener("click", () => setThread(control.dataset.thread));
    });

    document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
      if (event.key === "Escape") {
        setThread(caseNote.dataset.activeThread);
      }
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
